  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nLate tonight, a fairly disorganized area of showers and \nisolated thunderstorms were moving east along the I-70 \ncorridor, tied to a cold frontal zone. The disorganization is \nlargely due to lack of appreciable moisture and instability \n(MUCAPE 500 J/kg or less). This will be a continuing theme along\nthis frontal zone as it continues to move slowly south across \nsouthwest Kansas on Tuesday then again Wednesday. For this \nreason, there is a very low probability of severe storms with \nthis front, and even the chances of widespread rainfall amounts \nof 1/4\" or higher are dwindling in the forecast. Low level \nmoisture transport will favor central and south central Kansas, \nperhaps including our eastern counties, generally along/east of \nHighway 183 later Wednesday and Thursday Night. \n\nThe cold front later on today will will provide a nice respite \nfrom the heat, if only for a couple of days. Afternoon \ntemperatures today will likely track closer to the NBM 25th \npercentile given the fairly strong north-northeast winds and \nsome increased cloud cover (highs of around 80 at Scott City to \nWaKeeney). Along and south of the slow- moving front, upper 90s \nto near 100 are likely once again, generally southeast of a \nMeade to Larned line -- especially down in the typically hot Red\nHills region.\n\nThe frontal zone will likely be along the Oklahoma line late \nWednesday and lift slowly back north as a warm front Thursday, \nand as long as the front remains around our southwest Kansas \nregion, the temperature forecasting will continue to be a \nchallenge (mainly afternoon temperatures). NBM ranges 82 \nWaKeeney to 101 Medicine Lodge Wednesday with upper 90s/near 100\nadvancing slowly north into far southwest Kansas Thursday as \nthe frontal zone lifts back north. If the front bisects \nsouthwest Kansas from west-northwest to east- southeast, we will\nlikely see strong to marginally severe thunderstorms develop. \nThursday late afternoon/evening will likely be the best chance \nfor the most organized thunderstorm activity -- again mainly \neastern areas where dewpoints will be higher closer to central \nand south central Kansas.\n\nThis weekend into much of next week, the summer subtropical\nhigh will expand across the Rockies and central High Plains \nagain, favoring another 3+ day heat wave beginning as early as \nFriday in the southwest. Latest official forecast has at least \nthree-quarters of the DDC CWA 100+ highs beginning Sunday, \ncontinuing through Tuesday. Upper end high temperature potential\n(90th percentile NBM) shows some 105-106F highs across portions\nof mainly far southwest Kansas and also up toward Hays Sunday \nthrough Tuesday of next week.",
